tool_args.new_string:
To test whether the pipeline generalizes beyond SmolVLA, we evaluate on $\pi_{0.5}$~\cite{black2024pi0}, a 3.6B-parameter flow-matching VLA built on PaliGemma2 (vision) and Gemma2 (language) with 50-step action chunking. $\pi_{0.5}$ is $8{\times}$ larger than SmolVLA and uses a qualitatively different architecture (PaliGemma2 vision encoder vs.\ SmolVLA's SigLIP+Idefics3), providing a meaningful test of cross-VLA generalization.

\paragraph{Setup.}
We use the same LIBERO-Object and LIBERO-Spatial benchmarks with identical evaluation protocol (10 rollouts per task, 3 seeds). The CRM architecture and training procedure are unchanged from SmolVLA; only the input projection adapts automatically to $\pi_{0.5}$'s observation feature dimensionality ($2048$D vs.\ SmolVLA's $960$D) via a learned linear layer. This validates the pipeline's VLA-agnostic design: swapping the base VLA requires no architectural changes to the CRM beyond the input dimension.

\paragraph{Pipeline.}
Plan~A applies identically: $K{=}2$ samples from the frozen $\pi_{0.5}$, pairwise $L_2$ distance as the uncertainty probe, argmin selection, and selective CRM correction on high-uncertainty steps. The CRM is retrained on $\pi_{0.5}$'s rollout data using the same protocol (10 demos $\times$ 20 tasks, $K{=}10$ forward passes, $\varepsilon{=}0.2$).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Cross-VLA generalization: $\pi_{0.5}$ (3.6B) on LIBERO. The $\pi_{0.5}$ baseline operates at $81$--$85$\% SR, substantially above SmolVLA's $11$--$29$\%, constraining the headroom for correction. $n{=}3$ seeds.}
\label{tab:cross_vla}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Method & $K$ & Object (\%) & Spatial (\%) \\
\midrule
$\pi_{0.5}$ VLA-only & 1 & $81.5 \pm 0.5$ & $84.2 \pm 1.0$ \\
$\pi_{0.5}$ Plan~A & 2 & \placeholder{} & \placeholder{} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Baseline.}
Table~\ref{tab:cross_vla} reports results. The $\pi_{0.5}$ baseline already achieves $81.5 \pm 0.5$\% on Object and $84.2 \pm 1.0$\% on Spatial---substantially above SmolVLA's $11.0$\% / $29.4$\%---leaving limited headroom for post-hoc correction. This ceiling effect is informative: it tests whether the pipeline helps when the base policy is already strong, complementing the SmolVLA experiments where the base policy is weak. Plan~A results are pending (\placeholder{}).

